    {
      "title": "Centre treating Bihar as stepchild: Tejashwi Yadav on flood-related issues",
      "link": "https://www.thehindu.com/news/national/bihar/centre-treating-bihar-as-stepchild-tejashwi-yadav-on-flood-related-issues/article71463495.ece",
      "description": "The LoP in Bihar said that over 48 lakh people are affected by the recent floods, but the ‘double-engine’ government is not serious about this matter as both engines have caught fire",
      "source_name": "The Hindu National",
      "published": "2026-09-13T21:03:34"
    },
    {
      "title": "Over 4.8 mn affected in Bihar flood; water levels of rivers start to recede",
      "link": "https://www.business-standard.com/india-news/over-4-8-mn-affected-in-bihar-flood-water-levels-of-rivers-start-to-recede-126091300711_1.html",
      "description": "Over 48 lakh people remained affected in flood-hit Bihar on Sunday, even as waters started to recede in most rivers which had been flowing above the danger mark, officials said.\nBihar has received 111.7 mm of rainfall so far in September, around 13 per cent above normal, officials said.\nAround 48.70 lakh people in 575 gram panchayats across 88 blocks of 15 districts were affected by the deluge, they said.\nAmong the worst-affected districts are Patna, Bhojpur, Saran, Vaishali, Bhagalpur, Begusarai, and Araria, according to the Disaster Management Department (DMD).\nThe Kosi was flowing above the danger mark at Baltar and Kursela, the Burhi Gandak at Khagaria, the Ganga at Digha, Gandhi Ghat, Hathidah, Bhagalpur, Kahalgaon and Munger, the Punpun at Sripalpur, the Bagmati at Benibad and the Ghaghra at Darauli and Gangpur Siswan, the DMD said.\nThe administration is operating 1,257 community kitchens, and 13 relief camps are in flood-affected areas.\nArrangements for accommodation, food",
      "source_name": "Business Standard Latest",
      "published": "2026-09-13T18:05:09"
    }
  ]
